BT /F1 16 Tf 54 750 Td (Chapter 4: Cells, the Units of Life) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54 724 Td (\(Grade 8 life science course notes, written by Mr. Haddad for his class\)) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54 709 Td () Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54 694 Td (Every living thing is made of one or more cells. A cell is the smallest unit that can carry out all the) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54 679 Td (processes of life: taking in energy, getting rid of wastes, growing and reproducing.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54 664 Td () Tj ET
BT /F1 16 Tf 54 649 Td (4.1 The cell theory) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54 623 Td (Scientists in the 1600s and 1800s built the cell theory from many observations with microscopes.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54 608 Td (It has three parts:) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54 593 Td (1. All living things are made of one or more cells.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54 578 Td (2. The cell is the basic unit of structure and function in living things.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54 563 Td (3. All cells come from cells that already exist.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54 548 Td () Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54 533 Td (Robert Hooke named cells in 1665 after looking at thin slices of cork. Anton van Leeuwenhoek was the first to) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54 518 Td (see living single-celled organisms. Later, Schleiden \(plants\), Schwann \(animals\) and Virchow \(cells come from cells\)) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54 503 Td (put the pieces together.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54 488 Td () Tj ET
BT /F1 16 Tf 54 473 Td (4.2 Prokaryotes and eukaryotes) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54 447 Td (Prokaryotic cells, such as bacteria, have no nucleus; their DNA floats in the cytoplasm.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54 432 Td (Eukaryotic cells, such as plant, animal and fungus cells, keep their DNA inside a nucleus and have other) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54 417 Td (membrane-bound organelles. Prokaryotic cells are usually much smaller than eukaryotic cells.) Tj ET
endstream
